// @run
// @log 1
// @log 2
// @log 1
// @hint redundant type annotation: `c` is inferred as `{a: i32, b: i32}[]`
// @hint redundant type annotation: `rd` is inferred as `i32`
// A DECLARED INTERSECTION BESIDE THE STRUCTURAL SPELLING OF ITS OWN SHAPE, AT AN ARRAY
// ELEMENT (silent-class-inventory D402). Was a loud emit reject, `emitProgram: only i32[]
// arrays and struct/union element arrays are supported`, at TWELVE of sixteen structurally-
// spelled positions — a `const`, a `let`, a param, a return, a local, `[][]`, `.length`,
// `.push`, `for-in`, a global, a cross-function hand-off and an element write — but NOT at a
// struct field of the inline shape's list (read or written) or an un-annotated list, all
// three of which ran correctly. The fourth miss is where D442's silent cell lived.
//
// The declaration is inert without `AB`, a plain-struct `AB` runs, and a same-layout PLAIN
// struct twin used to RESCUE it, because the bridge then landed on a `TypeDecl` row — which is
// what named the gate. `shapeElemDeclaredStructIdx` resolved the inline element spelling to
// the `AB` row and then closed on `nameIsStructDecl(sNames[si])`, and a canonicalized object
// intersection is a one-variant `UnionDecl`, not a `TypeDecl`.
type AB = {a: i32} & {b: i32}
const c: {a: i32, b: i32}[] = [{ a: 1, b: 2 }]
function rd(xs: {a: i32, b: i32}[]): i32 { return xs[0].b }
print(c[0].a)
print(rd(c))
print(c.length)
